          oneLine: "R. Yochanan derives only Yom Kippur from 'to do, to atone'; R. Yehoshua b. Levi derives both heifer and YK. But the mishna explicitly mentions both separations - so R. Yochanan must hold heifer sequestering is only rabbinic (a maaleh).",
          shortSummary: "Rav Dimi reports a disagreement between leading Amoraim: R. Yochanan reads 'to do, to atone' as referring only to YK; R. Yehoshua b. Levi reads it as referring to both heifer ('to do') and YK ('to atone'). The Gemara challenges R. Yochanan's one-case position with the mishna's explicit mention of heifer sequestering. R. Yochanan resolves this: heifer sequestering is rabbinic only. A further challenge from a transmission in R. Yochanan's own name teaching two cases is resolved: that was his teacher R. Yishmael's view, transmitted through R. Yochanan but not his own.",
          whats: "Rav Dimi brings a report from Israel: R. Yochanan taught that the verse 'to do, to atone' derives only Yom Kippur from the inauguration; R. Yehoshua b. Levi taught it derives both the heifer and Yom Kippur. The Gemara immediately challenges R. Yochanan: the mishna itself lists heifer sequestering as equal to YK sequestering - doesn't that prove two cases? R. Yochanan's answer is terse: the heifer sequestering is a rabbinic enhancement (maaleh) without a Torah source. A further complication: a different chain of transmission quotes R. Yochanan himself as teaching two cases. Resolution: that transmission was in the name of his teacher R. Yishmael; when Ravin came from Israel, he clarified that R. Yochanan personally held only one case.",
          hint: "R. Yochanan's position has a significant consequence: heifer sequestering is only rabbinic while YK sequestering is Torah-level. What practical difference could this make?"
