          coreTension: "The cloud-period before God's call seems like a delay - but the Gemara insists it served a purpose. Three different purposes are proposed, each reflecting a different view of what Moses needed to be ready.",
          coreMove: "Each tanna identifies a different dimension of preparation: R. Akiva focuses on honor (public witness), R. Natan on physical transformation (like an angel), R. Matya on spiritual preparation (awe). They do not contradict each other - they address different layers of readiness.",
          resolution: "The three readings are complementary, not competing. Moses needed public recognition (R. Akiva), physical purification (R. Natan), and psychological awe (R. Matya). Rav Adda's addition integrates joy and trembling as the emotional tone of any genuine divine encounter.",
          takeaway: {
            type: "conceptual",
            text: "Preparation for encountering the Divine involves multiple dimensions: public legitimacy, physical readiness, and interior awe. The Psalm adds that joy and trembling are not opposites - they must coexist in genuine religious experience."
          },
