        lines: [
          { id: "yoma-004b-l01", kind: "gemara", he: "״וַיְכַסֵּהוּ הֶעָנָן״, לָהָר. ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה״ — [מֹשֶׁה]\nוְכׇל יִשְׂרָאֵל עוֹמְדִין, וְלֹא בָּא הַכָּתוּב אֶלָּא\nלַחְלֹק כָּבוֹד לְמֹשֶׁה. רַבִּי נָתָן אוֹמֵר: לֹא בָּא\nהַכָּתוּב אֶלָּא לְמָרֵק אֲכִילָה וּשְׁתִיָּה שֶׁבְּמֵעָיו,\nלְשׂוּמוֹ כְּמַלְאֲכֵי הַשָּׁרֵת.",
            vilna_line: 1, en: "And that which is written: <b>\"And the cloud covered him,\"</b> means the cloud covered it, <b>the mountain,</b> and not him, Moses. <b>\"And He called to Moses\"; Moses and all of the Jewish people were standing</b> at the foot of the mountain and listening, and if God did not mean that Moses was to climb the mountain, why did He call him? <b>The verse comes only to accord deference to Moses,</b> as the entire nation heard God address him. <b>Rabbi Natan says:</b> Moses was in fact called to enter the cloud; however, his entrance was not for the purpose of sequestering and purifying him, <b>rather, the verse comes only to cleanse the food and drink that was in his intestines, to render him like the ministering angels</b> who require neither food nor drink.", en_lit: "“And the cloud covered him,” the mountain, “And He called to Moses”; Moses and all of the Jewish people were standing The verse comes only to accord deference to Moses, Rabbi Natan says: rather, the verse comes only to cleanse the food and drink that was in his intestines, to render him like the ministering angels",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.4b.1", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-004b-l05", kind: "gemara", he: "רַבִּי מַתְיָא בֶּן חָרָשׁ\nאוֹמֵר: לֹא בָּא הַכָּתוּב אֶלָּא לְאַיֵּים עָלָיו, כְּדֵי\nשֶׁתְּהֵא תּוֹרָה נִיתֶּנֶת בְּאֵימָה, בִּרְתֵת וּבְזִיעַ,\nשֶׁנֶּאֱמַר: ״עִבְדוּ אֶת ה׳ בְּיִרְאָה וְגִילוּ בִּרְעָדָה״.\nמַאי ״וְגִילוּ בִּרְעָדָה״? אָמַר רַב אַדָּא בַּר מַתְנָה\nאָמַר רַב: בִּמְקוֹם גִּילָה שָׁם תְּהֵא רְעָדָה.",
            vilna_line: 5, en: "<b>Rabbi Matya ben Ḥarash says: The verse</b> calling Moses into the cloud <b>comes only to intimidate</b> Moses, to instill in him a sense of awe of the Creator, <b>so that the Torah would be delivered with reverence, with quaking and with trembling, as it is stated: \"Serve the Lord with awe, and rejoice with trembling\"</b> (Psalms 2:11). Apropos the end of the verse, the Gemara asks: <b>What</b> is the meaning of <b>\"and rejoice with trembling\"?</b> Joy and trembling seem contradictory. <b>Rav Adda bar Mattana said</b> that <b>Rav said: Where there is</b> the <b>joy of</b> fulfilling a mitzva, <b>there will be</b> the <b>trembling</b> of the awe of Heaven <b>there.</b>", en_lit: "Rabbi Matya ben Ḥarash says: The verse comes only to intimidate so that the Torah would be delivered with reverence, with quaking and with trembling, as it is stated: “Serve the Lord with awe, and rejoice with trembling” What “and rejoice with trembling”? Rav Adda bar Mattana said Rav said: Where there is joy of there will be trembling there.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.4b.2", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } }
        ],
