        display: {
          title: "Ravina adds rivui and meshicha; R. Yitzchak bar Bisna gives the source for R. Yochanan's principle",
          oneLine: "Ravina adds wearing priestly garments for seven days (rivui shiva) and anointing for seven days (meshicha shiva) to the list of divergences; a baraita proves any combination of these suffices; R. Yitzchak bar Bisna derives R. Yochanan's invalidation principle from 'kachah'; the daf ends open on where non-inauguration-passage requirements derive from.",
          shortSummary: "Ravina identifies two more practical differences: rivui shiva (wearing the full set of priestly garments across seven days) and meshicha shiva (anointing across seven days). A baraita on Lev. 16:32 ('the priest who shall be anointed and who shall be consecrated') proves that any combination of anointing and garment-wearing over seven days suffices. The need for anointing over seven days is derived from a juxtaposition: since garment-wearing requires seven days, and anointing is juxtaposed to garment-wearing, anointing also requires seven days. R. Yitzchak bar Bisna then gives the source for R. Yochanan's broad invalidation principle: the word 'kachah' (thus/so) in 'do thus to Aaron and his sons' means exact replication is required, making all explicitly written inauguration details indispensable. The daf ends with an open question: from where do we derive that inauguration requirements written elsewhere (not in the inauguration passage itself) also invalidate?",
          whats: "Ravina adds two more items to the list: rivui shiva (the Kohen Gadol wearing the full priestly garments on each of the seven days) and meshicha shiva (anointing him on each of the seven days). R. Yochanan holds omitting either invalidates; R. Chanina holds neither does, because neither invalidates ongoing service. A baraita on Lev. 16:32 establishes that any combination of anointing and garment-wearing over seven days makes a valid Kohen Gadol: Exod. 29:30 says 'seven days shall the son wear them,' implying that wearing garments for seven days - or anointing for seven days - or both - all suffice. The source that anointing requires seven days ab initio: it is juxtaposed to garment-wearing in a verse ('the sacred garments of Aaron shall be for his sons to be anointed in them and consecrated in them'), and since garment-wearing requires seven days, anointing does too. R. Yitzchak bar Bisna then derives R. Yochanan's principle: the word 'kachah' in 'do thus to Aaron and his sons' means exact replication, so every detail written in the inauguration passage must be observed exactly - and failure to do so invalidates. But 'kachah' only covers things explicitly written in the inauguration context. The final stub question: how do we derive that requirements written elsewhere (outside the inauguration passage) also invalidate? The daf ends here.",
          hint: "The word 'kachah' (thus) in a Torah command signals exact replication. What does this imply about inauguration requirements written in other contexts - like the priestly garment rules in Exodus 28?"
        },
