          shortSummary: "The Gemara already explained why the Kohen Gadol is sequestered in the Parhedrin chamber - derived from the inauguration or Sinai. But why must he leave his home (his wife)? The Gemara raises this as a distinct sub-question. R. Yehuda ben Beteira's answer: so he won't accidentally have relations with a safek niddah wife and become ritually impure before Yom Kippur. The Gemara corrects the phrasing: the concern is not that he will knowingly approach a safek niddah wife (that would assume wickedness). Rather: he might have relations innocently, and blood is later found on the sheets, creating uncertainty about whether she was a niddah during relations. This retroactive uncertainty would make him impure.",
          whats: "The sequestering question has two components. The first (why sequester at all?) was answered via R. Yochanan (from inauguration precedent) and Reish Lakish (from Sinai). The second (why specifically leave his wife?) is what this sugya addresses. R. Yehuda ben Beteira's concern is safek niddah - uncertain niddah status arising after the fact. If a woman finds blood after relations, and it is unclear whether she had already become a niddah during relations, her husband has potentially contracted seven-day impurity. The Gemara's correction is important: the Kohen Gadol is not expected to knowingly approach a safek niddah (he would not). The concern is that he acted innocently, and the discovery of blood afterward creates retroactive uncertainty. This is a standard legal mechanism: the law responds to what actually happened, not to the couple's intentions.",
