          whats: "The hutrah/dchuya distinction has precise practical implications. Hutrah: tumas meit in the context of communal Temple service is simply not prohibited - no more than eating matzah on Pesach is prohibited. Dchuya: it remains prohibited but is overridden by necessity - like a pikuach nefesh situation. The consequence: if dchuya, you must minimize exposure to the prohibited situation by seeking pure alternatives. If hutrah, no minimization is required. The first version of the dispute sets the threshold at 'same beit av': both agree pure priests serve when available within the unit; they disagree only on whether to go outside the unit. The second version is sharper: even within the same unit, does the impure priest have standing to serve alongside the pure one? Rav Nachman in the second version says yes - tumas meit is legally irrelevant for tzibbur service.",
