          coreTension: "The Rabbis generally required everyone to separate maaser sheni from dema'i grain. But bakers face a commercial reality: they sell continuously and are subject to government price controls enforced by officials (parhedrin) who show up every 12 months threatening them. Burdening them further with tithe accounting would be economically impossible.",
          coreMove: "Ulla's explanation introduces a sociological factor into halachic reasoning: when government pressure makes a community economically precarious, the Rabbis considered that factor when legislating tithe obligations. This is not halachic flexibility but a deliberate welfare consideration embedded in the ruling.",
