          oneLine: "R. Yochanan ben Torta explains why Shiloh fell - for giluy arayot and bizayon kodashim - but R. Shmuel bar Nachmani clarifies that Eli's sons did not literally commit adultery: they delayed women's bird-offerings, and scripture treats the delay as equivalent.",
          shortSummary: "R. Yochanan ben Torta: Shiloh was destroyed because of two sins practiced there - giluy arayot (sexual immorality) and bizayon kodashim (degradation of sacred offerings). On giluy arayot: R. Shmuel bar Nachmani in R. Yochanan's name says: anyone who says the sons of Eli sinned [in the sense of actual adultery] is simply wrong. The verse says they 'lay with the women who gathered at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting' - but in fact they delayed the processing of the women's bird-offerings (kanim). The women had to remain at the Tabernacle waiting, unable to return to their husbands. Scripture treats the delay as equivalent to the act of adultery, to underscore the severity of mishandling the Temple service.",
          whats: "This section closes the First Temple arc and pivots toward the 9b discussion of Temple destruction causes more broadly. The rehabilitation of Eli's sons is a key Talmudic device: holding biblical figures accountable for their actual sin (delay of offerings) rather than the worse sin the surface reading implies, while maintaining the moral gravity.",
