          oneLine: "The two sins of Eli's sons are detailed: delaying women's bird-offerings (treated by scripture as sexual immorality) and taking raw meat before the fat was burned (degradation of sacred offerings).",
          shortSummary: "Eli's sons' delay of women's bird-offerings: although they did not commit adultery, their delay kept women at the Tabernacle and thus separated from their husbands - scripture holds them accountable as if they had cohabited with the women. Their second sin: bizayon kodashim. Before the fat of an offering was burned on the altar, a servant of the priests would come and demand raw meat for roasting. The man bringing the sacrifice would protest: 'Let them first burn the fat today, then take whatever you wish.' The servant would say: 'No, give it now or I will take it by force.' This brazen taking of raw, unburned meat was the desecration.",
          whats: "This section closes the account of Shiloh's sins. Both sins are connected: the delay of bird-offerings kept women from purity and their husbands; the raw-meat taking showed contempt for the sacrificial order. Both involve the priests treating Temple service as an opportunity for personal benefit rather than divine service.",
          hint: "The raw-meat verse says they took 'before the fat was burned' - but more dramatically, before even the regular burning happened. The transgression is about sequence and disrespect, not just the taking of meat."
