          shortSummary: "The Second Temple generation engaged in Torah study, mitzvot, and acts of kindness. Why was the Temple destroyed? For sinat chinam (baseless hatred). This teaches that sinat chinam equals the three cardinal sins combined. Furthermore: the First Temple generation's sins were revealed (prophets could rebuke them) - their exile had a revealed end (70 years). The Second Temple generation's sins were hidden (sinat chinam does not appear as sin from the outside) - their exile has no revealed end. The lesson: hidden sin is more corrosive to communal life than open sin, because it cannot be identified, rebuked, or corrected.",
          whats: "This is the most famous passage in tractate Yoma. The sin of sinat chinam being equivalent to the three cardinal sins is not a claim about individual severity but about communal destruction capacity. The Temple requires a united people; sinat chinam makes unity impossible even among otherwise observant individuals.",
