          coreTension: "The three cardinal sins (avodah zarah, giluy arayot, shefichut damim) are those for which a Jew must die rather than violate them. Sinat chinam is not in that category. So how does the Talmud equate them? The answer is structural: those sins destroy individuals or relationships with God. Sinat chinam destroys the entire social fabric - every relationship, every community. The Temple requires a united people.",
          coreMove: "The comparison is communal destruction capacity, not individual severity. Pervasive sinat chinam prevents communal coherence, justice, and the social conditions that make Jewish life possible. One act of murder kills one person; pervasive hatred prevents the community from functioning at all.",
