          shortSummary: "What is sasmagor (the cedar-destroying rot mentioned by Reish Lakish)? Ulla explains: it is a boring creature that destroys cedar from within, eroding and hollowing its core. R. Abba then states: from when Haggai, Zechariah, and Malachi (the last prophets) died, the Ruach HaKodesh (prophetic spirit) ceased from Israel. Yet they still had the bat kol (heavenly voice) as a residual form of divine communication. Rav Pappa resolves the uncertainty: it was either Reish Lakish swimming with Zeira extending a hand, or Rabba bar bar Chana swimming with R. Elazar extending a hand. The identities are uncertain; the rebuke itself is the lesson.",
          whats: "This closing passage on 9b ties together the cedar/sasmagor metaphor with the bat kol theme: both are statements about spiritual diminishment. Prophecy ended with the last prophets; divine communication was reduced to the bat kol. This mirrors the Second Temple's diminished Shechina. The daf ends with ambiguity (who was in the Jordan?) as an acknowledgment that historical memory sometimes preserves the lesson without preserving every detail.",
