          oneLine: "The baraita concludes with three categories of mezuza-exempt structures: not-for-dwelling, not-for-honor, and sacred but non-residential.",
          shortSummary: "The baraita lists three grounds for mezuza exemption. First: gatehouse, portico, and balcony are exempt because they are not designated as dwellings - people pass through or rest there but do not live there. Second: bathroom, tannery, bathhouse, and mikveh are exempt because they lack the quality of honor (kavod) that a mezuza requires - undressing or undignified work makes the space inappropriate for the sacred text. Third: Temple Mount, Temple chambers and courtyards are exempt because they are sacred but not mundane residences. This third category is a definitive refutation of Rav Yehuda's position that the Parhedrin chamber mezuza was a Torah obligation.",
          whats: "The baraita that was cut off at the end of 11a now concludes on 11b. Three exclusion principles from the word 'house': (1) not designated for dwelling (gatehouse, portico, balcony); (2) not designed for honor (bathroom, tannery, bathhouse, mikveh); (3) sacred-but-not-residential (Temple precinct). The third is the decisive refutation of Rav Yehuda's claim that the Parhedrin chamber required a Torah-level mezuza.",
          hint: "The Gemara builds its list of exemptions by repeated application of the same verse - each time drawing a different inference from the word 'house.' The sacred/mundane distinction is the most unexpected: one might think a holier space deserves a mezuza, but sanctity and residential character are different categories."
