            vilna_line: 7, en: "I <b>might</b> have thought <b>that I include</b> in the obligation of <i>mezuza</i> <b>even</b> the <b>Temple Mount and</b> its <b>chambers and courtyards.</b> Therefore, <b>the verse states: House; just as a house</b> is a place <b>that is non-sacred, so too any</b> place <b>that is non-sacred</b> is obligated in the mitzva of <i>mezuza</i>, <b>excluding those</b> places <b>that are sacred.</b> For the purposes of this discussion, the <i>baraita</i> teaches that there are Sages who hold that barns and storehouses whose use is standard require a <i>mezuza</i>, contrary to the opinion of Rav Yehuda that everyone agrees that these structures are exempt. Consequently, the <i>baraita</i> is a <b>conclusive refutation</b> of his opinion and support for the contention of Rav Kahana that this matter is the subject of a tannaitic debate.", en_lit: "might that I include even Temple Mount and chambers and courtyards. the verse states: House; just as a house that is non-sacred, so too any that is non-sacred excluding those that are sacred. conclusive refutation",
