        display: {
          title: "The stabbing on the ramp: why one act of violence ended priestly competition for good",
          whats: "The incident that led to the lottery: once two priests were racing up the ramp and one pushed the other off, then stabbed him with a knife. The court was horrified. The father of the wounded priest (concerned it might make the knife ritually impure) caused a secondary incident. The Sages instituted the lottery system because of this violence.",
          hint: "The stabbing incident is shocking because it occurred in the Temple itself, by a priest, during service. The father's reaction (concerned about knife impurity!) adds a darkly comic element - even in crisis, halachic categories operated. The Sages rightly concluded that competitive systems in sacred contexts breed dangerous consequences."
        },
        learning: {
          learnerQuestion: "The race-and-count-off system in s01 sounds orderly enough to have been the mishna's default for generations. What happened that was severe enough to scrap it entirely rather than just patch it?",
          coreTension: "A procedure that had functioned as the accepted method for assigning ash removal had to be abolished outright and replaced system-wide, not just for this one service. That degree of reform implies the triggering event was not ordinary competitive friction but something the court saw as discrediting the whole approach.",
          coreMove: "Two priests racing up the ramp ended in one pushing the other and stabbing him with a knife. What turned this from a violent crime into grounds for comprehensive reform was the father's reaction: he ran up not principally to save his son but to remove the knife before it could contract corpse impurity if his son died while still impaled. That reflex, halachic categories operating even in the middle of a stabbing, showed the Sages how far competitive zeal had displaced ordinary human response, and they replaced racing with lottery for all Temple service assignments, not just this one.",
          resolution: "The Sages abolished the competitive race entirely and instituted the lottery (goralot) system for assigning all Temple services, not only ash removal, in direct response to this incident.",
          takeaway: {
            type: "logical_principle",
            text: "A single severe incident can discredit an entire procedural system even when that system had functioned acceptably for a long time, prompting comprehensive reform rather than a narrow patch."
          },
          ahaMoment: "The father's bizarre halachic concern mid-tragedy captures the Temple's unusual psychological environment: even at his son's stabbing, a priest's instinct was to prevent the knife from becoming impure. This reveals both the depth of halachic commitment and its potential to crowd out normal human responses.",
          learningBlocker: "Learners tend to treat the father's knife-impurity concern as a colorful side detail in a violence story, missing that it is the detail doing the real work: it is what showed the Sages that the competitive system itself, not just one bad actor, had distorted priestly judgment badly enough to require replacing it entirely.",
          memoryAnchor: "Priest stabbed on ramp. Father runs up, not to save his son, but to save the knife from impurity. Sages institute lottery for all Temple services."
        },
        lines: [
          { id: "yoma-022a-l36", kind: "gemara", he: "וּבִזְמַן שֶׁהֵן מְרוּבִּין\nוְכוּ׳. אָמַר רַב פָּפָּא: פְּשִׁיטָא לִי אַרְבַּע אַמּוֹת\nדְּאַרְעָא לָא, ״רָצִין וְעוֹלִין בַּכֶּבֶשׁ״ תְּנַן. קַמָּיָיתָא נָמֵי לָא, ״רָצִין וְעוֹלִין בַּכֶּבֶשׁ״ תְּנַן, וַהֲדַר\n״כׇּל הַקּוֹדֵם אֶת חֲבֵירוֹ״.",
            vilna_line: 36, en: "§ It was taught in the mishna that before the lottery was instituted, <b>when there were many</b> priests who sought to perform the removal of the ashes, the first priest to reach within four cubits of the top of the altar was privileged with performing the removal of the ashes. <b>Rav Pappa said:</b> It is <b>obvious to me</b> that the <b>four cubits</b> the mishna is referring to are not the four cubits adjacent to the ramp <b>on the ground,</b> because <b>we learned</b> in the mishna that the priests <b>run and ascend on the ramp,</b> and not adjacent to the ramp. It is <b>also not</b> referring to the <b>first</b> four cubits from the foot of the ramp, because <b>we learned</b> that the priests <b>run and ascend on the ramp, and only afterward</b> it says: <b>Any</b> priest <b>who precedes another</b> and reaches within four cubits of the altar first, indicating that the competition begins only once they have ascended the ramp to some extent.", en_lit: "when there were many Rav Pappa said: obvious to me four cubits on the ground, we learned run and ascend on the ramp, also not first we learned run and ascend on the ramp, and only afterward Any who precedes another",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.22a.11", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-022a-l39", kind: "gemara", he: "דְּבֵינֵי בֵּינֵי נָמֵי לָא, דְּלָא מְסַיְּימָא מִילְּתָא. פְּשִׁיטָא לִי דְּגַבֵּי\nמִזְבֵּחַ תְּנַן.",
            vilna_line: 39, en: "It is <b>also not</b> referring to four cubits somewhere in the <b>middle,</b> between the four on the bottom and the top of the altar, because <b>the matter is not defined</b> and there is no clear indication which four cubits on the ramp are the determining cubits. In light of all this, it is <b>obvious to me</b> that the four cubits <b>we learned</b> in the mishna are referring to the four cubits <b>that are adjacent to the altar</b> itself. The priest who reaches those four cubits first is the one privileged to remove the ashes.", en_lit: "also not middle, the matter is not defined obvious to me we learned that are adjacent to the altar",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.22a.12", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-022a-l40", kind: "gemara", he: "בָּעֵי רַב פָּפָּא: אַרְבַּע אַמּוֹת שֶׁאָמְרוּ, בַּהֲדֵי אַמָּה יְסוֹד וְאַמָּה סוֹבֵב,",
            vilna_line: 40, en: "Rav Pappa <b>raised a dilemma</b> based on the above clarification: Are the <b>four cubits that they stated,</b> which are the four cubits adjacent to the altar, calculated <b>including the cubit of the base</b> of the altar <b>and the cubit of</b> its <b>ledge,</b> as the ramp continues and overlaps these two cubits at the top of the altar,", en_lit: "raised a dilemma four cubits that they stated, including the cubit of the base and the cubit of ledge,",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.22a.13", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } }
        ],
