          memoryAnchor: "HP's bull: 'for himself and his household' - personal (Rava) or communal representative (Abaye)?",
          learnerQuestion: "Is the High Priest's Yom Kippur bull a communal offering brought on behalf of all Israel, or a personal offering for himself and his household?",
          coreTension: "Abaye, following R. Meir, holds the bull is a communal chatat because the High Priest represents all Israel; Rava holds it is personal, based on 'for himself and his household,' and the two positions rest on competing readings of the same verses and traditions.",
          coreMove: "The Gemara frames the dispute as turning on whose interests the High Priest represents when he brings the bull: Abaye with R. Meir reads the HP as a communal representative, while Rava reads 'for himself and his household' as the limiting scope, making the offering personal.",
