        lines: [
          { id: "yoma-053a-l38", kind: "gemara", he: "יָצָא וּבָא לוֹ דֶּרֶךְ\nכְּנִיסָתוֹ. מְנָא הָנֵי מִילֵּי? אָמַר רַבִּי שְׁמוּאֵל בַּר נַחְמָנִי, אָמַר רַבִּי יוֹנָתָן, אָמַר קְרָא: ״וַיָּבֹא\nשְׁלֹמֹה לַבָּמָה אֲשֶׁר בְּגִבְעוֹן יְרוּשָׁלִַם״, וְכִי מָה עִנְיַן גִּבְעוֹן אֵצֶל יְרוּשָׁלַיִם?",
            vilna_line: 38, en: "§ The mishna teaches that the High Priest <b>exits and comes</b> out <b>the way</b> that <b>he entered,</b> i.e., he does not turn around, but walks backward with his face toward the Ark. The Gemara asks: <b>From where are these matters</b> derived; what is the source in the Torah for this <i>halakha</i>? <b>Rabbi Shmuel bar Naḥmani said</b> that <b>Rabbi Yonatan said</b> that <b>the verse states: “And Solomon came to the high place that was at Gibeon, Jerusalem”</b> (II Chronicles 1:13). <b>Now, what does Gibeon have to do with Jerusalem?</b> Either he came to Gibeon or to Jerusalem; these are two different places.", en_lit: "exits and comes the way he entered, From where are these matters Rabbi Shmuel bar Naḥmani said Rabbi Yonatan said the verse states: “And Solomon came to the high place that was at Gibeon, Jerusalem” Now, what does Gibeon have to do with Jerusalem?",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.53a.21", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-053a-l40", kind: "gemara", he: "אֶלָּא:\nמַקִּישׁ יְצִיאָתוֹ מִגִּבְעוֹן לִירוּשָׁלַיִם, לְבִיאָתוֹ מִירוּשָׁלַיִם לְגִבְעוֹן: מָה בִּיאָתוֹ\nמִירוּשָׁלַיִם לְגִבְעוֹן — פָּנָיו כְּלַפֵּי בָמָה, כְּדֶרֶךְ בִּיאָתוֹ, אַף יְצִיאָתוֹ מִגִּבְעוֹן לִירוּשָׁלַיִם\n— פָּנָיו כְּלַפֵּי בָמָה, כְּדֶרֶךְ בִּיאָתוֹ.",
            vilna_line: 40, en: "<b>Rather,</b> the verse <b>compares his exit from Gibeon to Jerusalem to his arrival from Jerusalem to Gibeon. Just as</b> in the case of <b>his arrival from Jerusalem to Gibeon his face was</b> necessarily <b>turned toward the high place, in the usual manner</b> of a person’s <b>arrival, so too,</b> upon <b>his exit from Gibeon to Jerusalem his face</b> was still <b>turned toward the high place, in the manner of his arrival.</b> This teaches that one does not turn his face away from a sacred place; rather, he must walk backward.", en_lit: "Rather, compares his exit from Gibeon to Jerusalem to his arrival from Jerusalem to Gibeon. Just as his arrival from Jerusalem to Gibeon his face was turned toward the high place, in the usual manner arrival, so too, his exit from Gibeon to Jerusalem his face turned toward the high place, in the manner of his arrival.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.53a.22", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-053a-l43", kind: "gemara", he: "וְכֵן כֹּהֲנִים בַּעֲבוֹדָתָן, וּלְוִיִּם בְּדוּכָנָן, וְיִשְׂרָאֵל\nבְּמַעֲמָדָן, כְּשֶׁהֵן נִפְטָרִין — לֹא הָיוּ מַחֲזִירִין פְּנֵיהֶן וְהוֹלְכִין, אֶלָּא מְצַדְּדִין פְּנֵיהֶן וְהוֹלְכִין.",
            vilna_line: 43, en: "<b>And likewise,</b> with regard to <b>priests in their service; and Levites on their platform</b> in the Temple, where they recited songs; <b>and Israelites at their watches,</b> where they observed the sacrifice of the daily offering: <b>When they departed</b> from the sacred place, <b>they would not turn their faces and walk but would turn their faces sideways and walk,</b> so as not to turn their backs on the sacred place.", en_lit: "And likewise, priests in their service; and Levites on their platform and Israelites at their watches, When they departed they would not turn their faces and walk but would turn their faces sideways and walk,",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.53a.23", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-053a-l45", kind: "gemara", he: "וְכֵן תַּלְמִיד הַנִּפְטָר מֵרַבּוֹ לֹא יַחֲזִיר פָּנָיו וְיֵלֵךְ, אֶלָּא מְצַדֵּד פָּנָיו וְהוֹלֵךְ. כִּי הָא דְּרַבִּי\nאֶלְעָזָר כַּד הֲוָה מִיפְּטַר מִינֵּיהּ דְּרַבִּי יוֹחָנָן, כַּד הֲוָה בָּעֵי רַבִּי יוֹחָנָן לְסַגּוֹיֵי, הֲוָה גָּחֵין\nקָאֵי רַבִּי אֶלְעָזָר אַדּוּכְתֵּיהּ עַד דַּהֲוָה מִיכַּסֵּי רַבִּי יוֹחָנָן מִינֵּיהּ.",
            vilna_line: 45, en: "<b>And likewise, a student who takes leave of his teacher</b> should <b>not turn his face and walk but turn his face sideways and walk.</b> This is <b>in accordance with that</b> practice <b>of Rabbi Elazar when he took leave of his teacher, Rabbi Yoḥanan. When Rabbi Yoḥanan wanted to leave</b> him, <b>Rabbi Elazar would bend down and stand in his place</b> as a sign of respect and humility, <b>until Rabbi Yoḥanan disappeared from his sight;</b> only then would Rabbi Elazar turn to leave.", en_lit: "And likewise, a student who takes leave of his teacher not turn his face and walk but turn his face sideways and walk. in accordance with that of Rabbi Elazar when he took leave of his teacher, Rabbi Yoḥanan. When Rabbi Yoḥanan wanted to leave Rabbi Elazar would bend down and stand in his place until Rabbi Yoḥanan disappeared from his sight;",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.53a.24", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-053a-l47", kind: "gemara", he: "וְכַד הֲוָה בָּעֵי רַבִּי אֶלְעָזָר\nלְסַגּוֹיֵי, הֲוָה קָא אָזֵיל לַאֲחוֹרֵיהּ עַד דְּמִכַּסֵּי מִינֵּיהּ דְּרַבִּי יוֹחָנָן. רָבָא כַּד הֲוָה\nמִיפְּטַר מִינֵּיהּ דְּרַב יוֹסֵף, הֲוָה אָזֵיל לַאֲחוֹרֵיהּ עַד דְּמִנַּגְּפָן כַּרְעֵיהּ וּמִתַּוְּוסָן אִסְקוּפָּתָא דְּבֵי רַב יוֹסֵף דְּמָא.",
            vilna_line: 47, en: "<b>And when Rabbi Elazar wanted to leave, he would walk backward until he disappeared from Rabbi Yoḥanan’s sight,</b> and only then would he walk normally, so as not to turn his back on his teacher. The Gemara further relates: <b>When Rava took leave of Rav Yosef, he would walk backward,</b> paying no attention to the obstacles in his path, <b>until his legs were bruised and the threshold of Rav Yosef’s house was bloodied</b> from Rava’s wounds.", en_lit: "And when Rabbi Elazar wanted to leave, he would walk backward until he disappeared from Rabbi Yoḥanan’s sight, When Rava took leave of Rav Yosef, he would walk backward, until his legs were bruised and the threshold of Rav Yosef’s house was bloodied",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.53a.25", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } }
        ],
