        display: {
          whats: "Remaining blood poured at the outer altar's western base; blood service complete",
          hint: "After all sprinklings are complete, the HP pours the remaining blood from all services at the western base of the outer altar, concluding the complete four-zone Yom Kippur blood circuit.",
          title: "Remaining Blood Poured onto the Altar Base"
        },
        learning: {
          ahaMoment: "The blood completes its journey from the innermost (kapporet) to the outermost (outer altar base) - a full traversal of Temple sacred space in the service of atonement.",
          memoryAnchor: "Blood's circuit: from the Ark to the outer altar - the entire Temple purified in one Yom Kippur day.",
          learnerQuestion: "After all the sprinkling services are complete, what happens to the remaining blood in the bowls?",
          coreTension: "Blood used in Temple services cannot simply be discarded; there is a specific designated place for disposal, and the manner of disposal (pouring versus sprinkling) is itself a defined act.",
          coreMove: "The Talmud establishes that remaining blood from the Yom Kippur services is poured onto the base (yesod) of the outer altar; this base-pour is a disposal pour, not a sprinkling act, completing the blood service sequence.",
          takeaway: {
            type: "legal_principle",
            text: "Remaining blood from the Yom Kippur blood services is disposed of by pouring it onto the base (yesod) of the outer altar; this is a designated disposal act, not an additional sprinkling."
          },
          learningBlocker: "Students may confuse the base-pour with the sprinkling acts and count it as part of the ritual sprinkling sequence; the distinction between a sprinkling act and a disposal pour is important for understanding the total blood-act count."
        },
        lines: [
          { id: "yoma-057b-l27", kind: "gemara", he: "עֵירָה דַּם הַפָּר לְתוֹךְ\nדַּם הַשָּׂעִיר. תְּנַן כְּמַאן דְּאָמַר: מְעָרְבִין לִקְרָנוֹת.\nדְּאִיתְּמַר: רַבִּי יֹאשִׁיָּה וְרַבִּי יוֹנָתָן, חַד אָמַר:\nמְעָרְבִין, וְחַד אָמַר: אֵין מְעָרְבִין.",
            vilna_line: 27, en: "§ The mishna states that after the High Priest sprinkled the blood toward the curtain <b>he poured the blood of the bull into the blood of the goat.</b> The Gemara comments: <b>We learned</b> this mishna <b>in accordance with</b> the opinion of <b>the one who said</b> that the High Priest <b>mixes</b> the two types of blood before placing them <b>on the corners</b> of the inner altar, and he does not present each one separately. <b>As it was stated:</b> The later <i>tanna’im</i>, <b>Rabbi Yoshiya and Rabbi Yonatan,</b> debated this issue. <b>One</b> of them <b>said:</b> The High Priest <b>mixes</b> the blood for the purpose of placing the blood on the altar’s corners, <b>and one</b> of them <b>said: He does not mix</b> the blood.", en_lit: "he poured the blood of the bull into the blood of the goat. We learned in accordance with the one who said mixes on the corners As it was stated: Rabbi Yoshiya and Rabbi Yonatan, One said: mixes and one said: He does not mix",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.57b.12", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-057b-l30", kind: "gemara", he: "תִּסְתַּיֵּים דְּרַבִּי\nיֹאשִׁיָּה הוּא דְּאָמַר מְעָרְבִין, דְּאָמַר: אַף עַל גַּב דְּלָא\nכְּתִיב ״יַחְדָּיו״ — כְּמַאן דִּכְתִיב ״יַחְדָּיו״ דָּמֵי.",
            vilna_line: 30, en: "The Gemara comments: <b>Conclude</b> that <b>Rabbi Yoshiya was</b> the <i>tanna</i> <b>who said</b> that the High Priest <b>mixes</b> the blood. In general, he maintains that whenever a verse mentions two matters, <b>although</b> the term: <b>Together, is not written</b> explicitly, <b>it is considered as though</b> the term: <b>Together, is</b> indeed <b>written.</b> In other words, two items that appear in the same verse are treated as combined unless expressly stated otherwise. By contrast, Rabbi Yonatan maintains that even if two matters are mentioned together they are treated separately unless the verse uses the term: Together. In this case, the verse states: “And he shall take of the blood of the bull and of the blood of the goat” (Leviticus 16:18). Therefore, Rabbi Yoshiya rules that the High Priest must place the two sets of blood together.", en_lit: "Conclude Rabbi Yoshiya was who said mixes although Together, is not written it is considered as though Together, is written.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.57b.13", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-057b-l32", kind: "gemara", he: "אֲפִילּוּ\nתֵּימָא רַבִּי יוֹנָתָן הוּא, שָׁאנֵי הָכָא דִּכְתִיב: ״אַחַת״.",
            vilna_line: 32, en: "The Gemara rejects this contention. <b>Even</b> if <b>you say</b> that the mishna is in accordance with the opinion of <b>Rabbi Yonatan,</b> it remains possible to explain its ruling, since <b>it is different here, as it is written “once”</b> (Exodus 30:10), which indicates that there must be one act of sprinkling and not two. Consequently, the High Priest must mix the blood to ensure that there is only one presentation.", en_lit: "Even you say Rabbi Yonatan, it is different here, as it is written “once”",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.57b.14", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-057b-l34", kind: "gemara", he: "תַּנְיָא דְּלָא כְּשִׁנּוּיַין: ״וְלָקַח מִדַּם הַפָּר וּמִדַּם\nהַשָּׂעִיר״, שֶׁיִּהְיוּ מְעוֹרָבִין, דִּבְרֵי רַבִּי יֹאשִׁיָּה.",
            vilna_line: 34, en: "The Gemara comments: <b>It was taught</b> in a <i>baraita</i> <b>contrary to our response,</b> but in accordance with the initial assumption: “And he shall go out to the altar that is before the Lord, and make atonement for it; <b>and he shall take of the blood of the bull and of the blood of the goat,</b> and put it on the corners of the altar round about” (Leviticus 16:18). This verse teaches <b>that</b> the blood of the bull and the goat should <b>be mixed.</b> This is <b>the statement of Rabbi Yoshiya.</b>", en_lit: "It was taught contrary to our response, and he shall take of the blood of the bull and of the blood of the goat, that be mixed. the statement of Rabbi Yoshiya.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.57b.15", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } }
        ],
