        lines: [
          { id: "yoma-066b-l01", kind: "gemara", he: "שֶׁיְּהֵא מְזוּמָּן. ״עִתִּי״ — וַאֲפִילּוּ בְּשַׁבָּת, ״עִתִּי״ — וַאֲפִילּוּ\nבְּטוּמְאָה.",
            vilna_line: 1, en: "<b>that he should be designated</b> the day before. The word <b>appointed</b> also indicates that the scapegoat is always sent away at the appointed time, <b>and even on Shabbat.</b> Similarly, the word <b>appointed</b> indicates that the scapegoat is always sent away at the appointed time, <b>and even</b> when the appointed man is <b>in</b> a state of ritual <b>impurity.</b>", en_lit: "that he should be designated appointed and even on Shabbat. appointed and even in impurity.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.66b.1", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-066b-l02", kind: "gemara", he: "״אִישׁ״ — לְהַכְשִׁיר אֶת הַזָּר. פְּשִׁיטָא? מַהוּ\nדְּתֵימָא: ״כַּפָּרָה״ כְּתִיבָא בֵּיהּ, קָא מַשְׁמַע לַן.",
            vilna_line: 2, en: "The <i>baraita</i> stated that the word <b>man</b> is mentioned to <b>qualify a non-priest.</b> The Gemara expresses surprise: It is <b>obvious</b> that a non-priest is qualified for this service; why would one have thought otherwise? The Gemara answers: <b>Lest you say:</b> The term <b>atonement is written with regard to it,</b> and atonement is achieved only through services performed by priests. Therefore, <b>it teaches us</b> that this atonement is not achieved through a sacrificial offering, and consequently the service may be performed even by an Israelite.", en_lit: "man qualify a non-priest. obvious Lest you say: atonement is written with regard to it, it teaches us",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.66b.2", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-066b-l03", kind: "gemara", he: "״עִתִּי״ וַאֲפִילּוּ\nבְּשַׁבָּת. לְמַאי הִלְכְתָא? אָמַר רַב שֵׁשֶׁת: לוֹמַר,\nשֶׁאִם הָיָה חוֹלֶה — מַרְכִּיבוֹ עַל כְּתֵפוֹ.",
            vilna_line: 3, en: "The <i>baraita</i> stated that the word <b>appointed</b> indicates that the service is performed <b>even on Shabbat.</b> The Gemara asks: With regard <b>to what <i>halakha</i></b> is this stated? There is no apparent desecration of Shabbat by escorting the goat, since the <i>halakha</i> of Shabbat boundaries is merely Rabbinic. <b>Rav Sheshet said:</b> It is mentioned in order <b>to state that if</b> the goat <b>were ill</b> and could not walk the whole way, the one who escorts the goat <b>carries it on his shoulder.</b>", en_lit: "appointed even on Shabbat. to what halakha Rav Sheshet said: to state that if were ill carries it on his shoulder.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.66b.3", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-066b-l05", kind: "gemara", he: "כְּמַאן,\nדְּלָא כְּרַבִּי נָתָן. דְּאִי רַבִּי נָתָן, הָאָמַר: חַי נוֹשֵׂא\nאֶת עַצְמוֹ: אֲפִילּוּ תֵּימָא רַבִּי נָתָן, חָלָה שָׁאנֵי.",
            vilna_line: 5, en: "The Gemara comments: <b>In accordance with whose</b> opinion was this stated? It is <b>not in accordance with</b> the opinion of <b>Rabbi Natan, as, if</b> it were in accordance with the opinion of <b>Rabbi Natan, didn’t</b> he <b>say</b> that <b>a living</b> being <b>carries itself?</b> Because a living being is lighter than dead weight, the living being is considered to be aiding the one carrying it, and therefore carrying a living being is not considered an act of prohibited labor according to Torah law. The Gemara rejects this: <b>Even</b> if <b>you say</b> that it is in accordance with the opinion of <b>Rabbi Natan,</b> a living being that is <b>ill is different.</b> Since the goat cannot walk on its own strength, despite the fact that it is alive, all agree that the one who carries it is performing a prohibited labor.", en_lit: "In accordance with whose not in accordance with Rabbi Natan, as, if Rabbi Natan, didn’t say a living carries itself? Even you say Rabbi Natan, ill is different.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.66b.4", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-066b-l08", kind: "gemara", he: "אָמַר רַפְרָם, זֹאת אוֹמֶרֶת: עֵירוּב וְהוֹצָאָה\nלְשַׁבָּת, וְאֵין עֵירוּב וְהוֹצָאָה לְיוֹם הַכִּפּוּרִים.",
            vilna_line: 8, en: "Based on the fact that the word appointed indicates that the scapegoat is sent away even on Shabbat, <b>Rafram said: That is to say</b> that the concept of <b><i>eiruv</i> and</b> the prohibition against <b>carrying out</b> apply <b>to Shabbat,</b> but <b><i>eiruv</i> and carrying out do not</b> apply <b>to Yom Kippur.</b> If these <i>halakhot</i> applied equally to Yom Kippur, and nevertheless the Torah commanded that the scapegoat be sent away, it would be unnecessary to derive that the same is true even if Yom Kippur occurs on Shabbat.", en_lit: "Rafram said: That is to say eiruv and carrying out to Shabbat, eiruv and carrying out do not to Yom Kippur.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.66b.5", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-066b-l10", kind: "gemara", he: "״עִתִּי״ וַאֲפִילּוּ בְּטוּמְאָה. לְמַאי הִלְכְתָא? אָמַר רַב\nשֵׁשֶׁת: לוֹמַר שֶׁאִם נִטְמָא מְשַׁלְּחוֹ — נִכְנָס\nטָמֵא לָעֲזָרָה, וּמְשַׁלְּחוֹ.",
            vilna_line: 10, en: "The <i>baraita</i> stated that the word <b>appointed</b> indicates that the service is performed <b>even in</b> a state of ritual <b>impurity.</b> The Gemara asks: With regard <b>to what <i>halakha</i></b> is this stated? <b>Rav Sheshet said:</b> The verse comes <b>to tell</b> you <b>that if the one sending</b> the goat away <b>became impure, he</b> nevertheless <b>enters the</b> Temple <b>courtyard</b> while he is <b>impure and sends it</b> away.", en_lit: "appointed even in impurity. to what halakha Rav Sheshet said: to tell that if the one sending became impure, he enters the courtyard impure and sends it",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.66b.6", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } }
        ],
