        display: {
          whats: "Future river flowing from the Temple in the End of Days (Ezekiel 47)",
          hint: "The Gemara discusses the miraculous river that Ezekiel prophesied would flow from beneath the Temple threshold in the future, becoming a mighty river that heals the land.",
          title: "Ezekiel's Temple River"
        },
        learning: {
          ahaMoment: "The river's growth is a teaching device: holiness spreads from center to periphery, and what begins small at its source becomes overwhelming at the ends of the earth.",
          memoryAnchor: "Ankle, waist, swimming - the river grows outward; divine blessing expands from the Temple's center.",
          learnerQuestion: "What is the theological significance of the river that Ezekiel prophesied flowing from the Temple?",
          coreTension: "Between the river as a future eschatological vision and the Gemara's effort to understand its physical nature and symbolic meaning.",
          coreMove: "The Gemara reads the river's growth (ankle-deep to waist-deep to uncrossable) as a symbol of how holiness radiates from the Temple: small at the sacred center, vast at the periphery. The direction of flow embodies the principle of expanding divine blessing.",
          takeaway: {
            type: "conceptual",
            text: "Ezekiel's river teaches that divine blessing originates from the sacred center and expands outward; what begins as a trickle at the Temple threshold becomes an ocean of blessing at the far reaches."
          },
          learningBlocker: "Students may treat this as pure aggadah without seeing its theological function as a model for how holiness operates."
        },
        lines: [
          { id: "yoma-077b-l35b", kind: "gemara", he: "יָכוֹל יַעֲבִירֶנּוּ בְּסִיחוּי\n— תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר: ״כִּי גָאוּ הַמַּיִם מֵי שָׂחוּ״. מַאי ״מֵי שָׂחוּ״ — שִׁיּוּטָא, שֶׁכֵּן קוֹרִין לְשַׁיָּיטָא סַיָּיחָא. יָכוֹל\nיַעֲבִירֶנּוּ בְּבוּרְנִי קְטַנָּה — תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר: ״בַּל תֵּלֶךְ בּוֹ אֳנִי שַׁיִט״. יָכוֹל יַעֲבִירֶנּוּ בְּבוּרְנִי גְּדוֹלָה\n— תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר: ״וְצִי אַדִּיר לֹא יַעַבְרֶנּוּ״. מַאי מַשְׁמַע? כְּדִמְתַרְגֵּם רַב יוֹסֵף: לָא תֵּזִיל בֵּיהּ\nבִּסְפִינַת צַיָּידִין וּבוּרְנִי רַבְּתִי לֹא תְּגוּזִינֵּהּ.",
            vilna_line: 35, en: "§ Apropos the river that will flow in the future, the Gemara explains additional verses in Ezekiel. <b>One might think</b> that Ezekiel <b>could cross</b> the river <b>by swimming. The verse states: “For the water had swollen into <i>saḥu</i> waters”</b> (Ezekiel 47:5). <b>What</b> does <b>“<i>saḥu</i> waters”</b> mean? Water that can be traversed only with <b>a boat</b> [<b><i>shiyuta</i>], as sailing [<i>shayta</i>] is</b> sometimes <b>called swimming [<i>sayḥa</i>]. I might have thought</b> Ezekiel <b>could pass across in a small boat [<i>burni</i>].</b> Therefore, <b>the verse states: “No galley with oars shall go”</b> (Isaiah 33:21). I <b>might have thought he could pass through in a large boat.</b> Therefore, <b>the verse states: “Neither shall a fishing boat [<i>tzi adir</i>] be able to cross it”</b> (Isaiah 33:21). The Gemara asks: <b>From where</b> can it <b>be inferred</b> that the words <i>tzi adir</i> mean fishing boat? The Gemara explains: This is how <b>Rav Yosef translated</b> this verse: <b>A fishing boat [<i>sfinat tzayyadin</i>] will not travel on it and a large ship will not cross it.</b>", en_lit: "One might think could cross by swimming. The verse states: “For the water had swollen into saḥu waters” What “saḥu waters” a boat shiyuta], as sailing [shayta] is called swimming [sayḥa]. I might have thought could pass across in a small boat [burni]. the verse states: “No galley with oars shall go” might have thought he could pass through in a large boat. the verse states: “Neither shall a fishing boat [tzi adir] be able to cross it” From where be inferred Rav Yosef translated A fishing boat [sfinat tzayyadin] will not travel on it and a large ship will not cross it.",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.77b.15", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-077b-l39", kind: "gemara", he: "אָמַר רַבִּי יְהוּדָה בֶּן פַּזִּי: אַף מַלְאַךְ\nהַמָּוֶת אֵין לוֹ רְשׁוּת לַעֲבוֹר בְּתוֹכוֹ, כְּתִיב הָכָא: ״בַּל תֵּלֶךְ בּוֹ אֳנִי שַׁיִט״, וּכְתִיב הָתָם:\n״מִשּׁוּט בָּאָרֶץ״.",
            vilna_line: 39, en: "The Gemara continues its discussion of the river that will in the future come out of the Holy of Holies. <b>Rabbi Yehuda ben Pazi said: Even the Angel of Death does not have permission to pass through it</b> to the other side of this river, and proof of this is in the verse, <b>as it is written here: “No galley with oars [<i>ani shayt</i>] can travel” and</b> as <b>it was written there:</b> “Then Satan answered the Lord and said: <b>From going to and fro [<i>mishut</i>] the earth</b> and from walking up and down in it” (Job 1:7). Even Satan, who is also the Angel of Death, cannot cross through this river.", en_lit: "Rabbi Yehuda ben Pazi said: Even the Angel of Death does not have permission to pass through it as it is written here: “No galley with oars [ani shayt] can travel” and it was written there: From going to and fro [mishut] the earth",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.77b.16", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } },
          { id: "yoma-077b-l41", kind: "gemara", he: "אָמַר רַבִּי פִּנְחָס מִשּׁוּם רַב הוּנָא צִפּוֹרָאָה: מַעְיָן הַיּוֹצֵא מִבֵּית\nקׇדְשֵׁי הַקֳּדָשִׁים, בַּתְּחִילָּה דּוֹמֶה לְקַרְנֵי חֲגָבִים, כֵּיוָן שֶׁהִגִּיעַ לְפֶתַח הֵיכָל\n— נַעֲשֶׂה כְּחוּט שֶׁל שֶׁתִי, כֵּיוָן שֶׁהִגִּיעַ לָאוּלָם — נַעֲשֶׂה כְּחוּט שֶׁל עֵרֶב, כֵּיוָן\nשֶׁהִגִּיעַ אֶל פֶּתַח עֲזָרָה — נַעֲשֶׂה כְּפִי פַּךְ קָטָן, וְהַיְינוּ דִּתְנַן, רַבִּי אֱלִיעֶזֶר בֶּן יַעֲקֹב אוֹמֵר: מַיִם",
            vilna_line: 41, en: "The Gemara cites more <i>midrashim</i> about the river that will flow from the Holy of Holies. <b>Rabbi Pineḥas said in the name of Rav Huna of Tzippori: The spring that comes forth from inside the Holy of Holies</b> is <b>at first</b> very narrow <b>and resembles grasshoppers’ antennae</b> in width. <b>Once it reaches the opening of the Sanctuary it becomes</b> as thick <b>as</b> the <b>thread of</b> the <b>warp; once it reaches the Entrance Hall</b> of the Sanctuary, <b>it becomes</b> as thick <b>as</b> the <b>thread of</b> the <b>woof,</b> which is wider than the warp thread. <b>Once it reaches the opening of</b> the Temple <b>courtyard it becomes like the mouth of a small jug. This is as we learned</b> in a mishna: <b>Rav Eliezer ben Ya’akov says: Water,</b>", en_lit: "Rabbi Pineḥas said in the name of Rav Huna of Tzippori: The spring that comes forth from inside the Holy of Holies at first and resembles grasshoppers’ antennae Once it reaches the opening of the Sanctuary it becomes as thread of warp; once it reaches the Entrance Hall it becomes as thread of woof, Once it reaches the opening of courtyard it becomes like the mouth of a small jug. This is as we learned Rav Eliezer ben Ya’akov says: Water,",
            sefaria_ref: "Yoma.77b.17", commentaries: { rashi: [], tosafot: [] } }
        ],
