          memoryAnchor: "YK atones between person and God; for person-to-person sins, you must appease the one you wronged first.",
          learnerQuestion: "Why does Yom Kippur not atone for interpersonal sins unless the wronged person is directly appeased?",
          coreTension: "Between YK as a universal atonement day and the limitation that it cannot repair damage done to another person without that person's involvement.",
          coreMove: "The Gemara establishes: YK's atonement reaches the vertical dimension (between person and God) but not the horizontal (between persons) unless the wronged party is directly appeased. God cannot forgive what belongs to another person. The other person's forgiveness is a prerequisite that no ritual can bypass.",
